@@ -0,0 +1,13 @@
+# Carryover drafting: feasibility only
+
+No training was started. It requires a coordinator approval before any training run.
+
+The paper adds a learned marker for rejected verifier states, keeps them for one proposal only, and fine-tunes the drafter and its existing projections. Its recipe uses 500K recorded target responses and three continuation epochs; reported end-to-end improvements are 7.9–14.4% on its models with 15 proposed tokens. These are paper results, not DS41/M5 estimates. [Carryover Drafting](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14717)
+
+DS41 already returns taps from layers 37/38/39: 15,360 BF16 values, or 30,720 bytes per token. With the sprint's exactness constraint, proposals remain at most four tokens and verifier rows remain 2..5; disable changed drafting below 1,024 context tokens. Rejected features belong only to the drafter's temporary context and must never enter the committed target cache. Three stages' extra KV for four rejected rows is small; retaining rejected target taps adds 120 KiB per request before projection.
+
+A bounded pilot, if approved: 2,000 owner-approved mixed code/tool/prose prompts, 512 target tokens each, and 32 sampled anchors per response. Approximately 1.024M generated labels take 3.6–4.7 GPU-hours at 60–80 warm tok/s, excluding prefill. All recorded target taps occupy 29.3 GiB; four rejected rows at 64K anchors add at most 7.3 GiB. Store shared response features plus anchor offsets, not a separate 128-token feature window for every anchor. Detached verification of 64K anchors at roughly 40 ms per cycle costs another 43 minutes without batching gains.
+
+The resident target consumes about 224 GiB, so collect features under short leases, unload it, then train the drafter separately. The deployed quantized head is not a ready training model; a first experiment needs differentiable projection/adapter paths and either original weights or frozen quantized weights with adapters. The deployed three-stage draft tensors occupy 7.495 GiB on disk. The routed experts alone contain 13.59B logical weights (3 stages × 128 experts × 3 matrices × 5120 × 2304); a conventional 16-byte-per-parameter full-training state would require about 202.5 GiB for those experts alone, before attention, projections, activations, or the target. A small projection/adapter pilot is therefore the practical starting point on this Mac. Training throughput must be measured after approval; no reliable end-to-end training-time claim is available. Evaluate acceptance at matched anchors before spending an end-to-end lease, then require exact greedy HTTP texts at c1/c2 and a >=5% warm-server gain.
+
+Expected gain is uncertain: four-token proposals leave substantially less rejected suffix than the paper's 15-token setup. Treat 0–10% as a planning range, not a forecast. A cheap held-out acceptance study should decide whether a full pilot is justified. This is lower priority than the already validated cost-aware policy.
